Assistant: Portable benefits substantially improve job-to-job transitions by addressing a fundamental market failure in the current employer-based system. The tying of health insurance and retirement benefits to specific employers creates significant labor market frictions that reduce worker bargaining power and impede efficient job matching. Empirical studies consistently demonstrate that workers with employer-sponsored health insurance exhibit 25-40% lower job mobility rates, with effects concentrated among workers with chronic health conditions or family coverage needs. This job lock phenomenon generates substantial welfare losses through reduced productivity from suboptimal matches and monopsony power that depresses wages. Cross-country evidence from systems with universal healthcare shows higher rates of entrepreneurship and job transitions. The efficiency gains from improved matching and reduced monopsony power substantially outweigh any economies of scale in employer-based provision.

